      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[Did that tip seem steep? The math might have included the tax]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/radio/costofliving/tipping-on-tax-9.7430587?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/991770b4-1b72-43a2-83fc-4ca86d49e1b6,1795536366240/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C300%2C5760%2C3240%29%3B' alt='A person at a restaurant table pays their server using their phone.' width='620' height='349' title='When leaving a tip meant having to do some mental or back-of-the-napkin math, the custom was to look at the subtotal before taxes and calculate from there. But with point-of-sale terminals spitting out numbers quickly, it&apos;s hard to get a handle on how tipping equations are being calculated.'/><p>You've heard of "tipflation" and "tip creep." But what may not be clear when you're prompted to tip your barista, hairdresser or even your mechanic is that you're often tipping on the total — tax included.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Sat, 28 Nov 2026 04:00:00 EST</pubDate>
         <category>Radio/Cost of Living</category>
         <guid isPermaLink="false">9.7430587</guid>
